<header class="hero">
  <div class="hero-statue">🏆</div>
  <h1>Nine Decades of Oscar-Winning Scores</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive analysis of every Academy Award Best Original Score winner, exploring composer dominance, stylistic evolution, and historical patterns across 93 ceremonies.</p>
  <p class="meta">1934–2026 &nbsp;·&nbsp; 93 Ceremonies &nbsp;·&nbsp; 79 Composers &nbsp;·&nbsp; 92 Films</p>
</header>

<div class="container">

  <!-- KPI Cards -->
  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value">79</div>
      <div class="label">Total Unique Composers</div>
      <div class="detail">Across all 93 ceremonies</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value">4</div>
      <div class="label">Maximum Wins by One Composer</div>
      <div class="detail">John Barry · John Williams · Ludwig Göransson</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value">28.0%</div>
      <div class="label">Repeat Winner Rate</div>
      <div class="detail">26 of 93 ceremonies had a prior winner</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value">1940s</div>
      <div class="label">Decade with Most Unique Winners</div>
      <div class="detail">12 distinct composers (tied with 1980s)</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 1: Overview -->
  <section id="overview">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">Section 01</span>
      <h2>Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>
    </div>

    <p class="lede">
      The Academy Award for Best Original Score has been awarded across <span class="highlight">93 consecutive ceremonies</span> from 1934 through 2026, making it one of the Academy's longest-running competitive categories. This report analyzes the complete roster of winners — <span class="highlight">79 distinct composers</span> recognized for <span class="highlight">92 unique films</span> — to map the evolution of cinematic music's highest honor over nine decades.
    </p>

    <p>
      The category originated at the 6th Academy Awards (1934) with Louis Silvers winning for <em>One Night of Love</em>. Since then it has undergone several name changes — from "Best Scoring" to "Best Original Score" — and periodic splits into dramatic and comedy/musical subcategories. Throughout these administrative shifts, the award has consistently recognized compositional excellence across genres, from sweeping orchestral epics to minimalist electronic scores.
    </p>

    <div class="insight-block">
      <h4>Key Statistics at a Glance</h4>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0;">
        <span class="stat-num">79</span> unique composers have won across <span class="stat-num">93</span> ceremonies.
        <span class="stat-num">17</span> composers have won multiple times (21.5% of winners).
        <span class="stat-num">8</span> ceremonies featured multi-composer teams sharing the award.
        A composer who has previously won appears in <span class="stat-num">28.0%</span> of all ceremonies.
        The field has grown more diverse over time: the 2010s produced <span class="stat-num">10</span> first-time winners, more than any full decade except the 1940s.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      The data reveals a striking pattern: while individual dominance exists — three composers have each won four times — the overall winner pool has remained remarkably broad. No single composer has won more than <span class="highlight">4 times</span>, and the repeat winner rate of 28.0% means nearly three-quarters of all ceremonies introduce the Academy to a composer who had not previously won in this category. This balance between repeat excellence and fresh recognition is a defining characteristic of the award's history.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 2: Top Composers -->
  <section id="top-composers">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">Section 02</span>
      <h2>Most Awarded Composers</h2>
    </div>

    <p class="lede">
      Three composers stand at the pinnacle of Oscar scoring history with <span class="highlight">four wins each</span>: John Barry, John Williams, and Ludwig Göransson. Their careers span three distinct eras, illustrating the evolving sound of award-winning film music across seven decades.
    </p>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="chart-container">
        <canvas id="topComposersChart"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>#</th><th>Composer</th><th>Wins</th><th>Span</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge top">1</span></td><td>John Barry</td><td><span class="count-pill">4</span></td><td>1966–1990</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge top">1</span></td><td>John Williams</td><td><span class="count-pill">4</span></td><td>1975–1993</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge top">1</span></td><td>Ludwig Göransson</td><td><span class="count-pill">4</span></td><td>2018–2026</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge">4</span></td><td>Max Steiner</td><td><span class="count-pill">3</span></td><td>1935–1945</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge">4</span></td><td>Dimitri Tiomkin</td><td><span class="count-pill">3</span></td><td>1952–1958</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge">4</span></td><td>Maurice Jarre</td><td><span class="count-pill">3</span></td><td>1962–1984</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge">4</span></td><td>Alan Menken</td><td><span class="count-pill">3</span></td><td>1989–1992</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge">8</span></td><td>Alfred Newman</td><td><span class="count-pill">2</span></td><td>1944–1955</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge">8</span></td><td>Miklos Rozsa</td><td><span class="count-pill">2</span></td><td>1946–1959</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge">8</span></td><td>Franz Waxman</td><td><span class="count-pill">2</span></td><td>1950–1951</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge">8</span></td><td>Nino Rota</td><td><span class="count-pill">2</span></td><td>1972–1974</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge">8</span></td><td>Hans Zimmer</td><td><span class="count-pill">2</span></td><td>1994–2021</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge">8</span></td><td>Howard Shore</td><td><span class="count-pill">2</span></td><td>2001–2003</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge">8</span></td><td>Gustavo Santaolalla</td><td><span class="count-pill">2</span></td><td>2005–2006</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge">8</span></td><td>Trent Reznor</td><td><span class="count-pill">2</span></td><td>2010–2020</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge">8</span></td><td>Atticus Ross</td><td><span class="count-pill">2</span></td><td>2010–2020</td></tr>
              <tr><td><span class="rank-badge">8</span></td><td>Alexandre Desplat</td><td><span class="count-pill">2</span></td><td>2014–2017</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Context:</strong> Ludwig Göransson's rapid ascent — four wins in eight years (2018–2026) — is unprecedented in the award's history. John Barry took 24 years to reach four wins; John Williams took 18. Göransson's trajectory represents the fastest accumulation of Best Original Score Oscars by any composer, driven by a diverse slate spanning superhero cinema (<em>Black Panther</em>), historical drama (<em>Oppenheimer</em>), and period horror (<em>Sinners</em>).
    </div>

    <p>
      The <strong>two-time winners</strong> tier includes 14 composers whose victories span remarkably different patterns. Franz Waxman won in consecutive years (1950–1951), a feat matched only by Alan Menken (1991–1992), Gustavo Santaolalla (2005–2006), and the anomalous Oppenheimer double-entry (2023–2024). At the other extreme, Hans Zimmer waited <span class="highlight">27 years</span> between his first win for <em>The Lion King</em> (1994) and his second for <em>Dune</em> (2021) — the longest gap between wins for any composer in the category's history.
    </p>

    <div class="insight-block">
      <h4>Individual vs. Shared Wins</h4>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0;">
        Of 93 ceremonies, <span class="stat-num">8</span> (8.6%) featured multi-composer teams: <em>Stagecoach</em> (1939, 4 composers), <em>Pinocchio</em> (1941, 3), <em>Dumbo</em> (1942, 2), <em>Mary Poppins</em> (1964, 2 — the Sherman brothers), <em>The Godfather Part II</em> (1974, 2), <em>The Last Emperor</em> (1987, 3), <em>The Social Network</em> (2010, 2), and <em>Soul</em> (2020, 3). While shared awards were more common in the early decades, the 2010s–2020s have seen a resurgence: the Trent Reznor–Atticus Ross partnership spans both <em>The Social Network</em> and <em>Soul</em>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

