    <h3 style="color:var(--gold-light);margin:32px 0 16px;">Golden Age (1934–1959)</h3>
    <p>
      The Golden Age was defined by European émigré composers who brought late-Romantic orchestral traditions to Hollywood. <strong>Max Steiner</strong> (3 wins) and <strong>Dimitri Tiomkin</strong> (3 wins) defined the era's lush, theme-driven sound, while <strong>Erich Wolfgang Korngold</strong>'s single win for <em>The Adventures of Robin Hood</em> (1938) belies his enormous influence on the symphonic film score template. The era produced the most diverse winner pool of any period (25 unique composers in 26 ceremonies) — a reflection of the studio system's deep musical benches and the category's novelty. Only Franz Waxman achieved consecutive wins (1950–1951).
    </p>

    <h3 style="color:var(--gold-light);margin:32px 0 16px;">Silver Age (1960–1979)</h3>
    <p>
      The Silver Age marked a pivot toward more eclectic scoring styles. <strong>Maurice Jarre</strong> (2 wins), <strong>John Barry</strong> (2 wins), and <strong>John Williams</strong> (2 wins in this era — his first two of four) each brought distinctive voices: Jarre's impressionistic textures (<em>Lawrence of Arabia</em>, <em>Doctor Zhivago</em>), Barry's melodic romanticism (<em>Born Free</em>, <em>The Lion in Winter</em>), and Williams's leitmotif-driven orchestral power (<em>Jaws</em>, <em>Star Wars</em>). This era also saw the first pop-influenced winners: <strong>Burt Bacharach</strong> (<em>Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid</em>, 1969) and <strong>Giorgio Moroder</strong> (<em>Midnight Express</em>, 1978) signaled that the Academy's definition of "score" was broadening beyond traditional orchestral work.
    </p>

    <h3 style="color:var(--gold-light);margin:32px 0 16px;">Modern Era (1980–1999)</h3>
    <p>
