    </p>

    <h3 style="color:var(--gold-light);margin:32px 0 16px;">Contemporary Era (2000–2026)</h3>
    <p>
      The Contemporary Era has seen the greatest stylistic diversity in winning scores. <strong>Howard Shore</strong>'s operatic <em>Lord of the Rings</em> scores (2001, 2003) represent the apotheosis of the symphonic tradition, while <strong>Trent Reznor</strong> and <strong>Atticus Ross</strong> brought industrial and electronic textures to the winner's circle (<em>The Social Network</em>, <em>Soul</em>). <strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong> has emerged as the era's defining figure — four wins spanning Afrofuturist orchestral fusion (<em>Black Panther</em>), avant-garde historical scoring (<em>Oppenheimer</em>), and genre work (<em>Sinners</em>). The era also marked a milestone for representation: <strong>Hildur Guðnadóttir</strong> became the first solo woman to win the award for <em>Joker</em> (2019).
    </p>

